  "response": "교수님의 반응이 명확하지 않았다는 점이 오히려 더 마음을 불편하게 만드는 것 같아요. 표정이 읽히지 않으면, 그 빈칸을 우리 머릿속에서 가장 걱정스러운 해석으로 채우게 되니까요.\n\n그 빈칸을 채우면서 드는 생각은 보통 어떤 쪽인가요? 교수님이 제 실력을 낮게 보셨을 거라는 쪽인지, 아니면 그보다는 다른 쪽인지—혹시 두 가지가 섞여 있는지—어떤 느낌인지 들어볼 수 있을까요?",
  "reasoning": "The client is sharing a concern about a research lab meeting where they feel they didn't adequately explain the comparison criteria for their experiment. They're worried about how their professor perceived them, especially because the professor's reaction was unreadable - neither positive nor negative. The client is caught in uncertainty about what the professor thought.\n\nWhat's happening here:\n1. The client experienced a specific event (research meeting)\n2. They feel they didn't explain well enough\n3. The professor's reaction was ambiguous/unreadable\n4. They're now ruminating about what the professor might think\n5. The core pain seems to be the uncertainty - not knowing how they were perceived\n\nWhat would be helpful:\n- Acknowledge the specific pain of the ambiguous reaction - when someone's response is unreadable, our mind tends to fill in the worst\n- I could gently explore the nature of this worry - is it about the professor's judgment affecting their standing, or about feeling inadequate, or about the relationship with the professor?\n- I shouldn't jump to reassurance (\"probably the professor didn't think much of it\") because that dismisses their experience\n- I could reflect on how the ambiguity itself is what makes this so uncomfortable - the mind fills the void with the worst interpretation\n\nI think a good response would be to reflect on the specific discomfort of the ambiguous reaction - how not knowing can be harder than a clear negative response, because the mind fills in the blanks. This validates their experience without making assumptions about what the professor actually thought.\n\nLet me write this naturally in Korean, keeping it concise and warm.\n",
